1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,699
If you send a message to Voyager one right now, it will arrive tomorrow.

2
00:00:04,699 --> 00:00:05,324
Same time.

3
00:00:05,324 --> 00:00:06,824
Twenty four hours later.

4
00:00:06,824 --> 00:00:10,823
And if Voyager answers, you'll wait another full day to hear it.

5
00:00:11,523 --> 00:00:19,576
On November eighteenth, twenty twenty six, Voyager one became the first object humans ever built to reach one light-day from Earth.

6
00:00:19,576 --> 00:00:24,862
That's the distance light covers in a whole day, almost twenty six billion kilometers.

7
00:00:24,862 --> 00:00:29,657
A light-year, the unit we use for stars, is three hundred sixty five of these.

8
00:00:30,357 --> 00:00:32,468
So today we'll do something simple.

9
00:00:32,468 --> 00:00:36,147
We'll send one message, and ride along with it, hour by hour.

10
00:00:36,147 --> 00:00:40,128
And at every stop, we'll see what Voyager found when it got there.

11
00:00:40,828 --> 00:00:41,700
Zero seconds.

12
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:46,533
The message leaves a seventy meter antenna of NASA's Deep Space Network.

13
00:00:46,533 --> 00:00:50,224
That dish is as wide as a soccer field is long, almost.

14
00:00:50,224 --> 00:00:51,097
It has to be.

15
00:00:51,097 --> 00:00:55,057
Because what comes back from Voyager is unbelievably faint.

16
00:00:55,757 --> 00:00:57,263
One point three seconds.

17
00:00:57,263 --> 00:00:59,021
Our message passes the Moon.

18
00:00:59,021 --> 00:01:03,352
The Apollo astronauts needed about three days to make that same trip.

19
00:01:04,051 --> 00:01:05,915
Eight minutes and twenty seconds.

20
00:01:05,915 --> 00:01:08,287
We're now as far from Earth as the Sun is.

21
00:01:08,287 --> 00:01:11,731
Every sunrise you've ever seen was already eight minutes old.

22
00:01:12,432 --> 00:01:13,687
Forty three minutes.

23
00:01:13,687 --> 00:01:14,879
Jupiter's distance.

24
00:01:14,879 --> 00:01:20,026
Voyager one got here in March nineteen seventy nine, eighteen months after launch.

25
00:01:20,726 --> 00:01:22,262
Here's a strange detail.

26
00:01:22,262 --> 00:01:28,150
Voyager one is called one, but it launched second, sixteen days after its twin, Voyager two.

27
00:01:28,150 --> 00:01:30,966
It was simply put on a faster, shorter path.

28
00:01:30,966 --> 00:01:34,934
It overtook its sibling on the way, and reached Jupiter first.

29
00:01:35,634 --> 00:01:38,231
And it saw something no one expected.

30
00:01:38,231 --> 00:01:43,215
On Jupiter's moon Io, a plume rising hundreds of kilometers into space.

31
00:01:43,215 --> 00:01:44,619
An erupting volcano.

32
00:01:44,619 --> 00:01:48,200
The first active volcanoes ever found beyond Earth.

33
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:50,870
One hour and nineteen minutes.

34
00:01:50,870 --> 00:01:51,330
Saturn.

35
00:01:51,330 --> 00:01:52,973
November nineteen eighty.

36
00:01:52,973 --> 00:01:57,506
Here, the team made a choice that decided Voyager one's whole future.

37
00:01:57,506 --> 00:02:02,958
They sent it close past Titan, Saturn's giant moon, wrapped in a thick orange haze.

38
00:02:03,658 --> 00:02:05,107
The flyby was worth it.

39
00:02:05,107 --> 00:02:11,030
But Titan's gravity bent Voyager one's path up, out of the flat plane where the planets orbit.

40
00:02:11,030 --> 00:02:14,432
From that moment, it would never visit another planet.

41
00:02:14,432 --> 00:02:15,818
It was on its way out.

42
00:02:16,518 --> 00:02:18,230
Four hours and ten minutes.

43
00:02:18,230 --> 00:02:20,322
Neptune's orbit, the last planet.

44
00:02:20,322 --> 00:02:22,098
Voyager one never came here.

45
00:02:22,098 --> 00:02:22,922
Its twin did.

46
00:02:22,922 --> 00:02:31,291
In nineteen eighty nine, Voyager two flew past Neptune, and it is still the only spacecraft that has ever visited Uranus or Neptune.

47
00:02:31,291 --> 00:02:36,934
Every close-up picture of those two worlds, from any textbook, comes from that one visit.

48
00:02:37,634 --> 00:02:39,883
Five hours and thirty four minutes.

49
00:02:39,883 --> 00:02:41,360
Six billion kilometers.

50
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:48,877
On Valentine's Day, nineteen ninety, Voyager one turned its camera around, and took a family portrait of the planets.

51
00:02:49,577 --> 00:02:53,753
In one frame, Earth showed up inside a ray of scattered sunlight.

52
00:02:53,753 --> 00:02:57,158
It's the picture Carl Sagan called the Pale Blue Dot.

53
00:02:57,158 --> 00:03:00,563
Earth fills about twelve percent of one single pixel.

54
00:03:01,263 --> 00:03:06,778
Minutes after that portrait, the cameras were switched off for good, to save power and memory.

55
00:03:06,778 --> 00:03:10,650
Voyager one has not taken a picture in more than thirty six years.

56
00:03:11,350 --> 00:03:12,707
Nine and a half hours.

57
00:03:12,707 --> 00:03:22,822
In February nineteen ninety eight, at about this distance, Voyager one passed an older probe, Pioneer ten, and became the most distant object humans have ever made.

58
00:03:22,822 --> 00:03:25,042
Nothing has caught up with it since.

59
00:03:25,042 --> 00:03:27,756
Nothing we've launched is even on course to.

60
00:03:28,455 --> 00:03:29,391
Thirteen hours.

61
00:03:29,391 --> 00:03:31,822
Ninety four times farther than the Sun.

62
00:03:31,822 --> 00:03:36,186
In December twenty oh four, Voyager one crossed the termination shock.

63
00:03:36,186 --> 00:03:46,098
The solar wind, which blows out from the Sun at around four hundred kilometers per second, suddenly slows down here, and piles up, like a river hitting a lake.

64
00:03:46,798 --> 00:03:48,812
Sixteen hours and fifty minutes.

65
00:03:48,812 --> 00:03:51,645
The heliopause, the edge of the Sun's bubble.

66
00:03:51,645 --> 00:03:59,576
Voyager one crossed it on August twenty fifth, twenty twelve, at about one hundred twenty one times the Earth to Sun distance.

67
00:03:59,576 --> 00:04:02,031
Particles from the Sun almost vanished.

68
00:04:02,031 --> 00:04:04,297
Particles from deep space jumped up.

69
00:04:04,297 --> 00:04:08,451
For the first time, a human-made object was in interstellar space.

70
00:04:09,151 --> 00:04:14,837
Voyager two followed in twenty eighteen, on a different side of the bubble, a little closer in.

71
00:04:14,837 --> 00:04:18,129
That told scientists the bubble isn't a perfect sphere.

72
00:04:18,129 --> 00:04:24,533
Two probes, two crossings, and they are still the only direct measurements anyone has ever taken out there.

73
00:04:25,233 --> 00:04:26,344
Twenty four hours.

74
00:04:26,344 --> 00:04:28,072
Our message finally arrives.

75
00:04:28,072 --> 00:04:33,071
Voyager one is moving away from the Sun at about seventeen kilometers per second.

76
00:04:33,071 --> 00:04:35,848
More than sixty thousand kilometers per hour.

77
00:04:35,848 --> 00:04:40,477
Every year, it adds about three and a half times the Earth to Sun distance.

78
00:04:40,477 --> 00:04:45,414
At that pace, it will take about forty eight more years to reach two light-days.

79
00:04:46,114 --> 00:04:47,663
Now listen to the answer.

80
00:04:47,663 --> 00:04:52,804
Voyager's radio transmits with about twenty two watts, roughly a fridge light bulb.

81
00:04:52,804 --> 00:05:01,538
Spread over twenty six billion kilometers, our own estimate is that the dish on Earth catches less than a billionth of a billionth of a watt.

82
00:05:02,238 --> 00:05:03,573
And it arrives slowly.

83
00:05:03,573 --> 00:05:05,636
One hundred sixty bits per second.

84
00:05:05,636 --> 00:05:09,217
A single photo from your phone would take days to download.

85
00:05:09,217 --> 00:05:14,435
The computers that run the whole spacecraft have about sixty nine kilobytes of memory.

86
00:05:14,435 --> 00:05:19,411
A phone with a hundred twenty eight gigabytes holds almost two million times more.

87
00:05:20,111 --> 00:05:21,768
Even fixing it takes days.

88
00:05:21,768 --> 00:05:27,758
In late twenty twenty three, one memory chip failed, and Voyager one started sending nonsense.

89
00:05:27,758 --> 00:05:33,046
Engineers moved its code around, piece by piece, from twenty four light-hours away.

90
00:05:33,046 --> 00:05:35,786
Every test took two full days to come back.

91
00:05:35,786 --> 00:05:39,354
By April twenty twenty four, it was talking sense again.

92
00:05:40,054 --> 00:05:42,351
But there's a clock no one can fix.

93
00:05:42,351 --> 00:05:45,303
Voyager runs on heat from decaying plutonium.

94
00:05:45,303 --> 00:05:48,650
At launch, it had about four hundred seventy watts.

95
00:05:48,650 --> 00:05:51,209
It loses roughly four watts every year.

96
00:05:51,209 --> 00:05:53,046
Today it has less than half.

97
00:05:53,746 --> 00:05:57,827
So the team switches things off, one by one, to keep the rest alive.

98
00:05:57,827 --> 00:06:01,247
In April twenty twenty six, another instrument went dark.

99
00:06:01,247 --> 00:06:04,248
Voyager one launched with ten science instruments.

100
00:06:04,248 --> 00:06:10,549
Today, two are still on, listening to the magnetic field and the waves in the thin gas between the stars.

101
00:06:10,549 --> 00:06:17,810
In twenty twenty one, scientists reported that this gas has a faint, steady hum, picked up by the plasma wave instrument.

102
00:06:18,510 --> 00:06:19,443
So what's next?

103
00:06:19,443 --> 00:06:22,926
When the power finally runs out, Voyager one won't stop.

104
00:06:22,926 --> 00:06:26,720
It will keep coasting, silent, for longer than human history.

105
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:33,375
In about three hundred years, it should reach the inner edge of the Oort cloud, a vast shell of icy bodies.

106
00:06:33,375 --> 00:06:36,174
Crossing it could take thirty thousand years.

107
00:06:36,874 --> 00:06:44,841
In about forty thousand years, it will pass within one point six light-years of a small star called Gliese four forty five.

108
00:06:44,841 --> 00:06:47,367
That's the closest it gets to anything.

109
00:06:47,367 --> 00:06:48,144
Not a visit.

110
00:06:48,144 --> 00:06:49,439
Just a distant pass.

111
00:06:50,139 --> 00:06:51,919
And it carries a message too.

112
00:06:51,919 --> 00:06:58,483
A gold-plated record, with greetings in fifty five languages, sounds of Earth, and ninety minutes of music.

113
00:06:58,483 --> 00:07:00,017
A letter with no address.

114
00:07:00,717 --> 00:07:01,643
Now, the scale.

115
00:07:01,643 --> 00:07:04,794
Forty nine years of flight bought us one light-day.

116
00:07:04,794 --> 00:07:09,489
The nearest star, Proxima Centauri, is four point two four light-years away.

117
00:07:09,489 --> 00:07:11,959
That's about fifteen hundred light-days.

118
00:07:12,659 --> 00:07:17,656
At Voyager's speed, the trip to Proxima would take about seventy five thousand years.

119
00:07:17,656 --> 00:07:26,121
Voyager one is the fastest thing leaving our solar system, and it has covered less than one tenth of one percent of the way to the nearest star.

120
00:07:26,821 --> 00:07:28,249
So that's one message.

121
00:07:28,249 --> 00:07:29,352
Moon in a second.

122
00:07:29,352 --> 00:07:30,715
Sun in eight minutes.

123
00:07:30,715 --> 00:07:32,208
Jupiter in forty three.

124
00:07:32,208 --> 00:07:35,712
The edge of the Sun's bubble in under seventeen hours.

125
00:07:35,712 --> 00:07:39,606
And a tiny, half-asleep spacecraft at the end of a full day.

126
00:07:39,606 --> 00:07:46,810
NASA even asked people to flash the letters V G R in Morse code at the night sky, as a hello it will never see.

127
00:07:47,510 --> 00:07:50,922
Next time, we go much farther than any spacecraft ever will.

128
00:07:50,922 --> 00:07:53,310
Why can't we see the edge of the universe?

129
00:07:53,310 --> 00:07:55,869
It's not because our telescopes are too weak.

130
00:07:55,869 --> 00:07:58,541
Subscribe to Cosmic Scale so you don't miss it.

131
00:07:58,541 --> 00:08:02,635
And tell me in the comments, what would you put in a message to Voyager?
